1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 g6 5. Ng5 c6 6. Bd3 Bg7 7. f4 Nb4 8. Bc4 d5
9. Be2 Bf5 10. Na3 h6 11. Nf3 e6 12. c3 N4a6 13. O-O Nc7 14. Be3 Bf8 15. Nc2
Nd7 16. Bf2 Be7 17. Ne3 h5 18. b3 h4 19. Qd2 Be4 20. Rfd1 {Not best. 20.h3 was
better.} g5 {A dangerous move which is however Black's only real winning
chance in such a blocked position.} 21. Nxg5 Bxg5 22. fxg5 Qxg5 23. Bf1 Qg6 {
White threatened 24.Nxd5 and if ...Qxd2 25.Nxc7+ winning.} 24. Bd3 {Still 24.
h3 was better. But even then Black plays f5 with an excellent game.} f5 25.
Bxe4 {Now Black gets a dangerous pawn mass.} dxe4 {If 25...fxe4, White's
position is easy to defend.} 26. Nc4 O-O-O 27. Nd6+ Kb8 28. c4 {To prevent 28..
.Nd5.} Ne8 {Exchanging off White's strongly-posted knight.} 29. Nxe8 Rdxe8 {
29...Rhxe8 was better, with the threat of 30...Nxe5.} 30. h3 Reg8 31. Be3 c5 {
The only way to achieve anything.} 32. dxc5 Ka8 ({Of course, not} 32... Nxe5
33. Bf4 Qg7 34. Qb2 $1 {and wins.}) 33. c6 {The best chance. Black's king
becomes exposed.} Nxe5 {33...Bxc6 was simpler.} 34. cxb7+ Kxb7 35. Kh1 Nd3 36.
Rg1 ({Borngässer recommends here} 36. g4 {after which Black wins by} hxg3 {
etc. However, this is probably better than White's defence.}) 36... Ka8 {
A precaution or two before advancing the f and e pawns.} 37. c5 e5 38. c6 {
With the threat of 39.Bxa7.} Rh7 ({If} 38... Qxc6 39. Rac1 Nxc1 40. Rxc1 {
gives White chances.}) 39. Rac1 {There is now no good move. This is a last
trap as 39...Nxc1 40.Rxc1 is still playable. However...} f4 {There is now no
defence.} 40. c7 fxe3 {If now 41.c8=Q+ Rxc8 42.Rxc8+ Kb7 and White loses the
rook, since if 43.Qc2 or 43.Qc3 then 43...Nf2+ followed by Qg3 mate. Notes by
McKay in the tournament bulletin. This game was awarded the Best Game prize.
Source: Alan McGowan and Alistair Maxwell (Chess Scotland)} 0-1

